Subject: [PATCH 09/13] =?UTF-8?q?errors:=20report=20the=20remaining=20fail?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ures=20with=20x402=20v2=20=C2=A79=20codes,=20and=20answer=20unr?=
 =?UTF-8?q?eadable=20bodies=20in=20JSON?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Several errors still went out with x402-rs's own names, which are not x402 v2
§9 codes: invalid_payment_expired, invalid_payment_early, recipient_mismatch,
invalid_payment_amount, asset_mismatch, chain_id_mismatch,
accepted_requirements_mismatch, transaction_simulation and invalid_format.

- Exact scheme on EVM (v2 exact handler, at the same boundary that already
  maps signature failures): Early, Expired, InvalidPaymentAmount and
  RecipientMismatch become new PaymentVerificationError variants reported as
  invalid_exact_evm_payload_authorization_valid_after, ..._valid_before,
  ..._value_mismatch and invalid_exact_evm_payload_recipient_mismatch. Other
  chains and schemes keep the generic codes for these.
- Generic: InvalidFormat -> invalid_payload, ChainIdMismatch -> invalid_network,
  AssetMismatch -> invalid_payload (the payload authorizes another token),
  AcceptedRequirementsMismatch -> invalid_payment_requirements (the
  requirements are not the ones the payload accepted), TransactionSimulation
  -> invalid_transaction_state.
- A transfer simulation or gas estimate that reverts for any other reason
  (for example a paused token) becomes TransactionSimulation, i.e.
  invalid_transaction_state, instead of an unexpected error. Errors that are not
  reverts (an unreachable RPC) are still unexpected_*_error.
- A request that names no network now gets invalid_payload, and the /settle
  error omits network instead of sending "" when the request names none.
- A body that is not JSON gets the endpoint's JSON error shape with
  invalid_payload instead of axum's plain-text rejection (status unchanged).
